T:Big Drum, Small Drum
C:Grand Central Stationery (AI-assisted original, CC0)
O:Course original (no cultural attribution)
R:JK pulse and big/small contrast song
S:Grand Central Stationery, Ontario Listening Bridges (original course composition, 11 November 2026), CC0 1.0
N:Original JK pulse-and-contrast song; the class sings a low "big drum" call and a higher "small drum" call with a body sound in each rest, then answers the sung question "Big or small?" with one big or one small body sound in a silent bar, and chooses how loud the last BOOM is.
N:Listen: Kodo — "O-daiko", from Tsuzumi, https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uTJQV2-WRts. Watch the drummer's whole-body preparation before one big strike and the space after it; compare that one grounded sound with small, light taps.
N:Kodo is a professional taiko ensemble based on Sado Island, Niigata, Japan, performing staged ensemble drumming (kumi-daiko) worldwide since 1981; in the O-daiko the largest drum is played by a soloist whose stance, breath and stillness are part of the music. This is an original classroom pulse song for voices and body percussion, not taiko repertoire.
N:Original melody, rhythm and words; no Kodo passage, rhythm or choreography is copied or notated. The clip is a watching and listening reference only. Use classroom drums only after naming that they are not taiko.
N:Published early: an AI-assisted original written for this course and released CC0, not traditional and not by any artist named above. Not yet tried with a class or read by its reviewers (Early-years (JK) music teacher, taiko-informed educator, movement/accessibility reviewer for the stomp and tap choices, and a second score reader); its workshop record is kept for correction.
