T:Silver Drop
C:Grand Central Stationery (AI-assisted original, CC0)
O:Course original (no cultural attribution)
R:JK listening song with silent gaps
S:Grand Central Stationery, Ontario Listening Bridges (original course composition, 11 November 2026), CC0 1.0
N:Original JK listening song; the class sings a short falling "drop", then one child fills the silent bar with a soft bell or leaves it silent while everyone listens until the sound is gone; at the end the choice is a short wood sound or a long bell sound.
N:Listen: Evelyn Glennie — "A Little Prayer" (composed and played by Evelyn Glennie, solo marimba), from the album Rhythm Song (RCA Red Seal, 1990), https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6itfyUzl7KA. Listen past the strike into the fading sound; compare a wooden bar, a metal bell, a voice and silence.
N:Dame Evelyn Glennie is a Scottish solo percussionist and composer who has been profoundly deaf since childhood and describes listening through the whole body; "A Little Prayer", a slow marimba piece she wrote as a simple exercise, has since been arranged for many ensembles. This original sung sound study does not imitate her composition.
N:Original notes and words; the reference is named for listening only, linked from RCA Red Seal's auto-generated upload, with no audio or score embedded. No phrase of "A Little Prayer" is used.
N:Published early: an AI-assisted original written for this course and released CC0, not traditional and not by any artist named above. Not yet tried with a class or read by its reviewers (Early-years (JK) music teacher, accessibility-minded music educator (including Deaf or hard-of-hearing perspectives on the framing), classroom trial and a second score reader); its workshop record is kept for correction.
