T:Mirror Steps
C:Grand Central Stationery (AI-assisted original, CC0)
O:Course original (no cultural attribution)
R:SK partner movement song
S:Grand Central Stationery, Ontario Listening Bridges (original course composition, 11 November 2026), CC0 1.0
N:Original SK partner movement song; partners step on the beat side to side, turn and freeze in a chosen shape, then one shows a new step and the other answers it in the silent bar before the roles swap on "now you lead!".
N:Listen: Miriam Makeba — "Pata Pata" (stereo version), the 1967 Reprise single and title track of the album Pata Pata (1967), heard via the auto-generated Miriam Makeba channel from Strut Records' 2019 release, https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SBH_u-vEK30. Hear how repeated vocal and instrumental ideas make social movement feel communal; notice the leader's voice and the group's answer.
N:Miriam Makeba (1932-2008), a South African singer from Johannesburg, first recorded "Pata Pata" in South Africa in 1957; the song, sung partly in isiXhosa, names a township social dance ("pata pata", touch touch), and her 1967 US recording became an international hit. Banned from returning home in 1960 for speaking against apartheid, she lived in exile until 1990. Writing credits differ between sources (Makeba and Jerry Ragovoy on the 1967 release; some histories link the earlier song to Dorothy Masuka). This is a new movement song, not a "Pata Pata" arrangement.
N:Original melody, rhythm and English words; no hook, bass line, word or lyric from the recording is used, and the title no longer uses the word "pata". The recording is linked, not embedded.
N:Published early: an AI-assisted original written for this course and released CC0, not traditional and not by any artist named above. Not yet tried with a class or read by its reviewers (South African music educator (ideally an isiXhosa speaker), early-years (SK) music teacher, movement/accessibility reviewer, SK classroom trial and a second score reader); its workshop record is kept for correction.
