T:Pan Parade
C:Grand Central Stationery (AI-assisted original, CC0)
O:Course original (no cultural attribution)
R:SK parade song about pitch direction
S:Grand Central Stationery, Ontario Listening Bridges (original course composition, 11 November 2026), CC0 1.0
N:Original SK parade song about direction; the class marches and sings a line that climbs, one child plays a short up or down bell pattern in the gap, the class sings the line that falls, and a partner answers the first child's bells by copying or going the other way.
N:Listen: Lord Kitchener — "Pan in 'A' Minor", calypso for the 1987 Trinidad Carnival, heard on Kitchener, Forever Vol. 1 (Charlie's Records / VPAL Music, 2009) via the auto-generated Lord Kitchener channel, https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3SlzhJgJY9w. Listen for the bright family of steelpan tone colours and the way a calypso can celebrate the instrument and its players.
N:Steelpan was created in Trinidad and Tobago in the mid-twentieth century, when players in Port of Spain's neighbourhoods turned oil drums into tuned instruments, and grew through community steel orchestras and the Panorama competition. Lord Kitchener (Aldwyn Roberts, 1922-2000) wrote many calypsos about pan that steel orchestras chose for Panorama, "Pan in 'A' Minor" among them in 1987. This original classroom parade song uses voices and barred instruments; it is not calypso and not a steelband score.
N:Original tune and words; no line, melody or rhythm from Lord Kitchener's copyrighted song is used, and the song is in a major key, not minor. The recording is linked, not embedded.
N:Published early: an AI-assisted original written for this course and released CC0, not traditional and not by any artist named above. Not yet tried with a class or read by its reviewers (Trinidadian steelpan educator, early-years (SK) music teacher, SK classroom trial and a second score reader); its workshop record is kept for correction.
