C:Grand Central Stationery (AI-assisted original, CC0)
O:Course original (no cultural attribution)
R:SK partner breath-and-silence song
S:Grand Central Stationery, Ontario Listening Bridges (original course composition, 11 November 2026), CC0 1.0
N:Original SK breath-and-silence song for partners; one partner sings the rising call to a long tone, everyone breathes in the silent bar, and the other partner answers with the longer phrase that comes home, choosing words or a hum.
N:Listen: Riley Lee — "Tōri (Passing)", traditional solo shakuhachi honkyoku, live at the Cygnet Folk Festival, Tasmania, January 2024 (Riley Lee Music, the artist's official YouTube channel, published 24 February 2025), https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gubxoukY-pQ. Listen to breath as part of the tone and to silence as part of the phrase, not as an empty mistake.
N:Riley Lee, an American-born shakuhachi player based in Australia, became in 1980 the first non-Japanese player to receive the rank of dai shihan (grand master). The shakuhachi is a Japanese end-blown bamboo flute; honkyoku ("original pieces") such as "Tōri" come from the Fuke Zen Buddhist tradition, where breath and silence are part of the practice. This new vocal study is not honkyoku and does not imitate a sacred piece.
N:Original melody and words; no pitch sequence, ornament or breath pattern is taken from the reference, which is unmetred solo flute music. The reference is linked from the artist's own channel, not embedded.
N:Published early: an AI-assisted original written for this course and released CC0, not traditional and not by any artist named above. Not yet tried with a class or read by its reviewers (Shakuhachi-informed educator (ideally a teacher in a honkyoku lineage), early-years (SK) music teacher, SK classroom trial and a second score reader); its workshop record is kept for correction.
