T:Water Groove
T:Original Grade 1 groove and call-and-response song
C:Grand Central Stationery (AI-assisted original, CC0)
O:Course original (no cultural attribution)
R:Grade 1 groove song with call and response
S:Grand Central Stationery, Ontario Listening Bridges (original course composition, 11 November 2026), CC0 1.0
N:Original Grade 1 groove song for call and response and for feeling beat against rhythm; bars 1-2 become an ostinato layer once the song is known.
N:Listen: Fela Kuti and Africa 70 — "Water No Get Enemy" (1975), https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kisTH3SFegc. Track one repeating part while other layers enter; hear that a groove can stay steady while the surface changes.
N:Fela Anikulapo Kuti (1938-1997) built Afrobeat in Lagos, Nigeria, from Yoruba music, highlife, jazz and funk, with long pieces in which drums, guitars, bass and horns lock together in layers and his words, in Nigerian Pidgin and Yoruba, criticise power. "Water No Get Enemy" (1975) takes its title from a proverb about water's strength. This three-note classroom song is a first lesson in keeping one part going, not an Afrobeat piece.
N:Original melody, rhythm and words; no horn line, lyric, proverb wording, bass pattern or harmony from the recording is used. The words mention water only as a picture of something that keeps moving.
N:Published early: an AI-assisted original written for this course and released CC0, not traditional and not by any artist named above. Not yet tried with a class or read by its reviewers (Grade 1 classroom music teacher, Nigerian or Afrobeat-informed music educator, second score reader); its workshop record is kept for correction.
