T:Sing It Up, Sing It Down
T:Original Grade 1 contour song
C:Grand Central Stationery (AI-assisted original, CC0)
O:Course original (no cultural attribution)
R:Grade 1 contour song in question and answer
S:Grand Central Stationery, Ontario Listening Bridges (original course composition, 11 November 2026), CC0 1.0
N:Original Grade 1 contour song: the words say where the melody goes (up, down, float, fall, home) so children connect high and low sounds with the shape of a phrase, and hear smooth against detached singing.
N:Listen: Cesária Évora — "Sodade", from the album Miss Perfumado (Lusafrica, 1992); linked upload is Lusafrica's 2012 remaster for the 20th Anniversary Edition, https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Mo4dmYifj0U. Follow the rise and fall of a sung phrase and notice how a restrained voice can carry strong feeling.
N:Cesária Évora (1941-2011), from Mindelo on the island of São Vicente, Cabo Verde, sang morna and coladeira in Cabo Verdean Kriolu and became known worldwide after Miss Perfumado (1992). Morna is Cabo Verde's slow song form, bound up with the islands' long history of emigration and the feeling called sodade; the song "Sodade" speaks of the road to São Tomé. This contour song is not a morna and does not borrow the song.
N:Entirely original melody, rhythm and English words on three notes; no lyric, chord sequence, rhythmic figure or title phrase from "Sodade" is reproduced, and the piece no longer takes the song's title.
N:Published early: an AI-assisted original written for this course and released CC0, not traditional and not by any artist named above. Not yet tried with a class or read by its reviewers (Grade 1 classroom music teacher, Cabo Verdean music educator, second score reader); its workshop record is kept for correction.
