T:Original Grade 1 call-and-response song for voices and percussion
C:Grand Central Stationery (AI-assisted original, CC0)
O:Course original (no cultural attribution)
R:Grade 1 call and response for voices and percussion
S:Grand Central Stationery, Ontario Listening Bridges (original course composition, 11 November 2026), CC0 1.0
N:Original Grade 1 call-and-response game song in which a leader's sung call is answered first by the class's voices and then by two percussion groups.
N:Listen: Lucho Bermúdez y Su Orquesta — "Colombia Tierra Querida" (CBS recording, ℗ 1972 CBS, Inc.), reissued on La Cumbia Colombiana (Sony Music Entertainment Colombia, 2025), https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JMBKvLh3gWQ. Listen for a clear two-beat dance pulse, contrasting instrumental colours and a melody that invites a group response.
N:Lucho Bermúdez (1912-1994), clarinettist, composer and bandleader from El Carmen de Bolívar, brought the porro and cumbia of Colombia's Caribbean coast into big dance-orchestra arrangements that were danced across Colombia and abroad; "Colombia Tierra Querida" became one of the country's best-loved songs. Cumbia grew from Indigenous, African and Spanish lineages on that coast (drums, gaitas, maracas, the scraper called guacharaca). This is a new 2/4 classroom tune, not an arrangement and not a cumbia rhythm.
N:Original notes, rhythm and words; no melody, lyric, horn line or arrangement from the recording is copied, and the classroom percussion answers are free choices, not cumbia patterns.
N:Published early: an AI-assisted original written for this course and released CC0, not traditional and not by any artist named above. Not yet tried with a class or read by its reviewers (Grade 1 classroom music teacher, Colombian music educator, second score reader); its workshop record is kept for correction.
