T:Mbira Mirror
T:Original Grade 2 mirror song over a bordun
C:Grand Central Stationery (AI-assisted original, CC0)
O:Course original (no cultural attribution)
R:Grade 2 AB mirror song with bordun
S:Grand Central Stationery, Ontario Listening Bridges (original course composition, 11 November 2026), CC0 1.0
N:Original Grade 2 AB song in which the second half is the first half sung backwards, over a do-so bordun that repeats like a cycle.
N:Listen: Stella Chiweshe — "Chachimurenga", from the album Ambuya? (Piranha, 1987); linked upload is Piranha Records' remastered version from the 2021 reissue Ambuya!, https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2k9GM7vloIA. Follow one repeating interlocking figure while the voice moves independently; notice cyclical rather than chord-by-chord listening.
N:Stella Chiweshe (1946-2023) was one of the first women to become a professional player of the mbira dzavadzimu, the Shona lamellophone of Zimbabwe used in ceremonies that call on the ancestors, and she carried it to audiences worldwide. "Chachimurenga", the opening track of Ambuya? (1987), takes its name from chimurenga, the Shona word for struggle used for Zimbabwe's liberation wars. In mbira music interlocking parts repeat in cycles while voices weave over them. This original pentatonic school piece is not mbira repertoire.
N:Original melody, words and bordun; no kushaura or kutsinhira (mbira lead or interlocking) part, lyric or recording phrase is transcribed. The mirror form is a classroom device, not a feature of mbira music.
N:Published early: an AI-assisted original written for this course and released CC0, not traditional and not by any artist named above. Not yet tried with a class or read by its reviewers (Grade 2 classroom music teacher, Zimbabwean mbira educator, second score reader); its workshop record is kept for correction.
