N:Original two-part classroom round and listening bridge; the class learns one eight-bar melody in unison, then group 2 starts it again when group 1 reaches bar 5, so a single line is carried by many voices before the class listens to "Mannenberg".
N:Listen: Dollar Brand (Abdullah Ibrahim) — "Mannenberg", recorded in Cape Town in 1974 with Basil Coetzee on tenor saxophone, produced by Rashid Vally, https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YsCWDGCQGRk. Hear a memorable line become a shared anthem as the ensemble repeats, answers and intensifies it.
N:"Mannenberg" takes its name from Manenberg, a Cape Flats township built for families forcibly moved under apartheid's Group Areas Act; the 1974 recording became an anthem of the struggle against apartheid. This original 3/4 school round does not reproduce its groove, melody or harmony.
N:Original metre, notes and words. The round's 3/4 pentatonic lines share no riff, rhythm, contour, harmony or arrangement with the recording's 4/4 theme or Basil Coetzee's solo.
