N:Original secular call-and-response classroom song and listening bridge; a leader sings each question and the class answers, then everyone sings the quiet middle together before the calls return; the class then listens for how a lead singer and a party answer one another in qawwali.
N:Listen: Nusrat Fateh Ali Khan and Party — "Allah Ho Allah Ho", recorded live at WOMAD, Mersea Island, Essex, England, 20 July 1985; released on "Live at WOMAD 1985" (Real World Records, 23 July 2019), https://realworldrecords.com/releases/live-at-womad-1985/. Listen to lead voice and party answer, repetition that gathers energy, handclaps and virtuosic variation.
N:Qawwali is a Sufi devotional performance tradition of Pakistan and North India, sung by a lead singer and a party at shrines and gatherings; it is worship, not simply a vocal style. "Allah Ho" repeats the name of God. This secular original teaches musical conversation and must not be presented as qawwali.
N:Original tune and neutral English words; no devotional text, sacred name, melodic phrase, rhythm cycle or harmonium pattern is borrowed, and no student is asked to sing or imitate the recording.
