N:Original Grade 3 partner song and listening bridge; the class sings A, B and A in unison, then group 2 sings the B line against group 1's returning A, before comparing how Mercedes Sosa uses breath, timbre and phrase shape.
N:Listen: Mercedes Sosa — "Gracias a la vida" (words and music by Violeta Parra), from Homenaje a Violeta Parra, https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=C-WgW_4ilv0. Notice how vocal timbre, breath and phrase shape make a repeated metre carry a public message.
N:Violeta Parra wrote "Gracias a la vida" in 1966, near the end of her life; Mercedes Sosa's 1971 recording carried it across the Spanish-speaking world and it became a defining song of nueva canción, the movement that joined folk forms to social testimony. This original partner song does not use its words, melody or harmony.
N:No lyric, melody, rhythm or harmony from the copyrighted song is used; the classroom piece is a pentatonic partner song with new English words, and the reference is for listening and historical inquiry.
