T:Arc of the Night
T:Original expressive study for soloist and ensemble
C:Grand Central Stationery (AI-assisted original, CC0)
O:Course original (no cultural attribution)
R:instrumental expressive study and listening bridge
S:Grand Central Stationery, Ontario Listening Bridges (original course composition, 11 November 2026), CC0 1.0
N:Original Grade 4 instrumental expressive study and listening bridge; a soloist states a phrase and reshapes it, the ensemble answers and rises to a climax, and the line settles to a held cadence, before the class listens to how Umm Kulthum and her orchestra stretch and answer a line.
N:Listen: Umm Kulthum — "Alf Leila wa Leila" (words by Morsi Gamil Aziz, music by Baligh Hamdi, 1969); play a short excerpt, https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8bCC3EMUifo. Hear how a singer reshapes a line, how the ensemble answers, and how expectation can build across a long performance.
N:Umm Kulthum (c. 1904-1975) was Egypt's most celebrated singer; her monthly concerts, broadcast across the Arab world, stretched one song over an hour as she reshaped each line in answer to her listeners. That practice can introduce Arabic art music, maqam (here maqam Farahfaza, related to Nahawand) and tarab, the shared state of musical rapture. This short equal-tempered original is not a maqam composition.
N:Original pitches and no Arabic text; no phrase, rhythm or orchestration of the song is copied. The first draft's E flat over D, a stock "Middle Eastern" signifier, was removed; the only accidental is a C sharp leading note, and the piece claims no maqam.
N:Published early: an AI-assisted original written for this course and released CC0, not traditional and not by any artist named above. Not yet tried with a class or read by its reviewers (Arab music educator, Grade 4 teacher and second score reader); its workshop record is kept for correction.
