N:Original Grade 4 call-and-chorus song with a clapped second part, and a listening bridge; a leader or small group sings each call, the class answers with an accented chorus while a clapping group holds a syncopated ostinato, before listening to how Ilê Aiyê's chorus and drums carry identity.
N:Listen: Ilê Aiyê — "Que Bloco É Esse?" (Paulinho Camafeu), from Canto Negro (1984), https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aV5ezQ0gQSE. Listen for massed percussion, a declarative chorus and identity carried through ensemble sound.
N:Ilê Aiyê, founded on 11 November 1974 in the Curuzu neighbourhood of Liberdade, Salvador, Bahia, was the first bloco afro: a Carnival group open only to Black members, created in answer to racism in Salvador's Carnival and dedicated to Black Brazilian and African identity. "Que Bloco É Esse?" was its first Carnival song. This original school chorus is not samba-reggae, not a bloco afro song and not an Ilê Aiyê arrangement.
N:Original tune, words and clapped ostinato; no chorus, lyric, drum part, rhythm pattern or arrangement from the recording is copied, and the ostinato is a plain classroom syn-co-pa, not a samba-reggae pattern.
N:Published early: an AI-assisted original written for this course and released CC0, not traditional and not by any artist named above. Not yet tried with a class or read by its reviewers (Afro-Brazilian or Bahia music educator, Grade 4 teacher and second score reader); its workshop record is kept for correction. The Band claps the ostinato (eighth, quarter, eighth, quarter rest, quarter: cp cp _ cp ~ ~ cp _); a clapping group learns it from the Band line.
