N:Original sung listening-bridge song for Grade 6, a counting song that makes 3+2 audible: the class sings the melody, a small group plays the ostinato, a body-percussion group chooses a grouping, and a soloist answers with a named interval in the rests.
N:Listen: The Dave Brubeck Quartet — "Take Five" (composed by Paul Desmond), on the album Time Out (Columbia, 1959), https://www.davebrubeck.com/dave-brubecks-time-out. Feel 5/4 as a repeating grouping, then hear how a solo can range freely while the ostinato stays dependable.
N:Paul Desmond composed "Take Five" for the Dave Brubeck Quartet. This original 5/4 study uses a different melody, harmony and grouping material.
N:No riff, piano vamp, chord progression, solo language, lyric or drum pattern from "Take Five" is copied; the ostinato is a plain root-and-fifth walk, and the form is ABA', not the reference's; credit Desmond as composer in the lesson.
